    question:"What explains the unity of anything composed of parts?",
    thesis:"Composite things exist only while their constituents are unified. Their derivative unity points to an absolutely simple, noncomposite cause—the One.",
    objectives:["Recognize several kinds of composition","Explain why composition calls for a concurrent cause","Distinguish simplicity from smallness","Follow the derivation from the One to intellect and power","Compare this proof with the Aristotelian route"],
    vocabulary:[["Composite","Made actual through distinguishable constituents, principles, or aspects."],["The One","The absolutely noncomposite source of all composite reality."],["Concurrent cause","A cause required while an effect exists, not merely at its beginning."],["Divine simplicity","The thesis that God is not assembled from metaphysical or physical parts."],["Participation","A dependent thing’s limited possession of a feature derived from a source."]],
    summary:[
      ["Composition as the clue","The proof starts from ordinary things as composites. Composition need not mean visible mechanical pieces. A thing may be composed of matter and form, essence and existence, genus and difference, substance and accidents, or properties organized into one subject. Whatever the type, the constituents do not explain by themselves why they form this one existing whole. The unity of the composite is an additional fact needing an account."],
      ["Why a concurrent unifier is required","If the cause of a composite’s unity were itself composite, its own unity would stand in need of explanation. The resulting series is hierarchical because every member’s ability to unify is borrowed here and now. Merely adding more derivative unifiers never yields a source of unity. The explanatory terminus must therefore be absolutely noncomposite. This is not the smallest particle; particles have structure and differentiating features. It is metaphysically simple."],
      ["The One and the divine attributes","Two absolutely simple causes would need some differentiating feature, but possessing one feature rather than another would introduce composition. The source is therefore unique. Because change involves gaining or losing some actuality and hence complexity across states, the simple source is immutable; because beginnings and endings involve composition or dissolution, it is eternal. It is neither material nor an inert abstraction. Feser argues that the remaining category is mind, and that the cause of all composite being must contain the perfections of its effects without receiving them as parts."],
      ["The distinctive contribution","The Aristotelian proof begins with change and arrives at pure act; this proof begins with composition and arrives at absolute simplicity. They converge but expose different explanatory pressures. The important defensive move is to keep ‘simple’ metaphysical. The issue is not computational simplicity, low information, or few physical components, but the absence of ontological constituents whose combination would itself need explanation."]
    ],
    chain:[["Observe","Experienced things are composite in one or more senses."],["Explain","A composite exists only while its constituents are combined."],["Regress","A composite unifier would itself require a unifying cause."],["Terminate","A hierarchical series of borrowed unity requires an absolutely noncomposite source."],["Unify","Absolute simplicity excludes multiplication, change, temporal limits, and materiality."],["Identify","The unique simple cause of all else is the One and possesses the core divine attributes."]],
    misconceptions:["Absolute simplicity does not mean spatial smallness.","The proof is not an argument from biological design.","A collection of simple physical particles would still involve composition.","Calling the cause ‘the One’ is a metaphysical conclusion, not a numerical slogan."],
