    vocabulary:[["Abstract object","A universal, proposition, number, possible world, or similar non-particular object of thought."],["Nominalism","The view that universals or abstract objects are merely names, collections, or linguistic constructions."],["Conceptualism","The view that abstract objects exist only as concepts in minds."],["Scholastic realism","The view that universals exist in things, in finite intellects, and paradigmatically in the divine intellect."],["Necessary truth","A truth that could not have been otherwise."]],
    summary:[
      ["The datum: objective abstract structure","Mathematical truths, propositions, universals, and possibilities appear objective and necessary. Two plus two would equal four even if nobody believed it; contradictory propositions cannot both be true merely by convention. The chapter surveys nominalist efforts to reduce such objects to words, inscriptions, classes, resemblance, or concrete particulars and argues that these accounts presuppose the very common natures or truth conditions they aim to eliminate."],
      ["Why finite conceptualism is not enough","Conceptualism locates abstracta in thought, which captures their intelligibility, but finite minds are contingent, fallible, and temporally limited. Necessary truths do not become true when a human thinks them, nor cease when every human forgets them. A merely human conceptualism therefore cannot ground their necessity or complete systematic relations. This motivates a necessarily existing intellect rather than a contingent population of thinkers."],
      ["Why not a freestanding Platonic realm?","Platonic realism protects objectivity by positing abstract objects independently of matter and mind, but Feser presses problems of causal inertness, unity, and intelligibility. A disconnected ‘third realm’ does not explain why abstracta form a single logically ordered system, how they relate to concrete things, or why minds grasp them. Scholastic realism treats them as divine ideas: not entities floating beside God, but patterns comprehended in a necessary intellect."],
      ["From necessary intellect to God","The interlocking system of truths and possibilities requires one conceptually omniscient intellect. Because it exists necessarily, it lacks unrealized existential potential and connects with the earlier route to pure act. Pure actuality in turn excludes change, materiality, and limitation, and supports omniscience in the stronger sense that includes contingent truths. The chapter’s force depends on the prior metaphysics of abstract objects; a good critic must engage that debate rather than simply insist that numbers are inventions."]
    ],
    chain:[["Observe","Necessary truths and universals are objective and not reducible to particular marks or sounds."],["Eliminate","Nominalism and finite-mind conceptualism fail to preserve their necessity and unity."],["Compare","A mind-independent third realm leaves relation, unity, and intelligibility unexplained."],["Ground","The full system of abstracta exists as ideas in a necessarily existing intellect."],["Unify","The system is interlocking, supporting one conceptually omniscient intellect."],["Identify","Necessary intellect connects to pure act and therefore the classical divine attributes."]],
