  {
    id:7, title:"Common Objections to Natural Theology", pages:"249–307", duration:"3 hours", icon:"⚖",
    question:"Which objections strike the actual arguments, and which attack substitutes?",
    thesis:"Most popular objections fail by mis-stating the proofs, confusing metaphysical with scientific explanation, or ignoring the derivation of divine attributes. Serious criticism must identify and reject a real premise.",
    objectives:["Diagnose straw-man objections quickly","Explain why causal principles are limited rather than exception-ridden","Separate metaphysical from scientific explanation","Respond carefully to evil and divine hiddenness","Practice charitable, premise-focused persuasion"],
    vocabulary:[["Straw man","A weakened substitute attacked instead of the actual argument."],["Fallacy of composition","Invalidly transferring a property of parts to a whole without warrant."],["Scientism","The claim that science is the only genuine or reliable route to knowledge."],["God of the gaps","An appeal to divine action inserted where a future scientific explanation might go."],["Theodicy","An account of God’s reasons for permitting evil, distinct from a defense of logical consistency."],["Divine hiddenness","The objection that a loving God would make his existence more evident to all nonresistant persons."]],
    summary:[
      ["‘What caused God?’ and special pleading","None of the five proofs begins with ‘everything has a cause.’ Each identifies a specific condition requiring explanation: potency, composition, received existence, contingency, or the objective system of ideas. The terminus lacks that condition. Asking what actualizes pure act or composes the absolutely simple misapplies the rule. A critic may deny that such a terminus is coherent or demonstrated, but must argue for that denial rather than repeat the question."],
      ["Infinite regresses, beginnings, and composition","Most arguments in the book do not assume that the universe began. They target hierarchical dependence now. An infinite temporal series may be granted for the sake of argument. Nor do the proofs simply infer that because each part has a cause the universe has one. The issue is whether a series made only of derivative members can supply the causal or explanatory power every member borrows. The rationalist argument’s concern with the total contingent situation also needs separate analysis rather than a reflex charge of composition fallacy."],
      ["Science, laws, and ‘God of the gaps’","A scientific explanation identifies mechanisms, regularities, and interactions within the natural order. These proofs ask what makes change, composition, existence, law-governed powers, and intelligibility possible at all. A deeper physical theory can replace an earlier physical account but still presupposes actuality, causal powers, and existence. The point is not to plug an empirical ignorance with God. Scientism—the rule that only science yields knowledge—is either not itself a scientific result or is defined so broadly that philosophy counts as part of rational inquiry."],
      ["Complex designer and ‘one god further’","The Hume–Dawkins complexity objection is aimed at design arguments that infer a designer with enough information to arrange biological or cosmic complexity. Feser’s arguments conclude instead that the ultimate cause is metaphysically noncomposite. The ‘one god further’ slogan equivocates between mythological beings within the cosmos and the classical-theist source of all being. Rejecting Zeus as a powerful embodied agent does not supply a reason to reject pure act, just as rejecting drawn imperfect triangles does not refute geometry."],
      ["Evil and suffering","The logical problem asks whether God and evil are strictly inconsistent; a theodicy asks what reasons God might have. Feser argues that goods such as courage, mercy, freely formed character, and a stable natural order can require the permission of certain evils, and that evil is privation rather than a created positive substance. The evidential problem has more force, but its inference from ‘we see no justifying reason’ to ‘there is none’ must be weighed against independent arguments for God and the limits of human perspective. This is not a complete pastoral answer to suffering, and persuasion requires humility."],
      ["Hiddenness and intellectual responsibility","The hiddenness argument assumes a perfectly loving God would ensure obvious belief and that such belief is absent through no fault of the nonbeliever. Feser questions both premises and separates bare belief that God exists from freely willing relationship with God. He also notes that widespread religious belief complicates claims of universal hiddenness. The chapter ends by asking both sides to examine motivated reasoning: the psychological origin of a belief neither proves nor disproves its content. Arguments stand or fall on premises and inferences."],
      ["A better apologetic method","First restate the objection in its strongest form. Second name the exact proof and premise under attack. Third distinguish the conclusion of natural theology from later revealed claims. Fourth answer only what follows from the relevant premises. Fifth admit the argument’s real pressure points. The goal is understanding and rational persuasion, not scoring points. A listener is more likely to reconsider when they can recognize their own concern in your summary."]
    ],
    chain:[["Clarify","Name the exact proof and state it without slogans."],["Locate","Ask which premise or inference the objection denies."],["Distinguish","Separate temporal from hierarchical causation and scientific from metaphysical explanation."],["Answer","Show why the objection misses, or defend the challenged premise."],["Concede","Acknowledge the strongest remaining uncertainty or cost."],["Invite","Return the conversation to the best version of the critic’s real disagreement."]],
    misconceptions:["A bad objection does not prove the argument sound.","Explaining metaphysical dependence does not compete with physical mechanisms.","A logical defense concerning evil is not automatically a full theodicy.","Psychological motives are not logical refutations.","Persuasion is not the same as overwhelming someone with premises."],
    quizzes:[
      {q:"What is wrong with ‘everything has a cause, so what caused God?’",a:["Nothing; it refutes every proof","The five arguments use restricted explanatory principles and derive a terminus lacking the relevant condition","God is exempt by definition only","Causes do not exist"],correct:1,why:"Each proof specifies why derivative realities need a cause and why the terminus is non-derivative."},
      {q:"Why is a future physical theory not automatically a rival?",a:["Physics is unreliable","Metaphysical arguments ask about presuppositions of any physical theory","Theology predicts equations","Natural causes are illusions"],correct:1,why:"Mechanisms within nature and the ontological ground of nature answer different questions."},
      {q:"What does the logical problem of evil attempt to show?",a:["Some suffering feels pointless","God and evil are strictly inconsistent","Every evil has a known purpose","Religion causes harm"],correct:1,why:"Logical inconsistency is stronger than evidential improbability and requires a contradiction."},
      {q:"What is the best response pattern?",a:["Win quickly with a slogan","Identify the actual premise, steelman the concern, answer narrowly, acknowledge limits","Change the subject","Quote an authority"],correct:1,why:"Premise-focused charity improves both rigor and persuasion."},
      {q:"Why does the ‘one god further’ slogan equivocate?",a:["All religions are identical","Classical God is not one embodied member of a class of deities","Myths are historically recent","Polytheists deny causality"],correct:1,why:"The categories and explanatory roles are radically different."}
    ],
    drills:["Turn ‘Who made God?’ into the strongest premise-level objection you can formulate.","Role-play a scientist who sees metaphysics as a gap-filler; answer while affirming scientific explanation.","Give a compassionate response to the evidential problem of evil before giving a philosophical one.","Explain the difference between Zeus and classical theism without insulting a believer or skeptic.","Hold a seven-minute dialogue in which your only goal is to find the exact disputed premise."],
    videoPrompt:"Create a 15-minute objection clinic. Put each objection on screen, label whether it attacks a real premise or a straw man, and answer it in three layers: 20 seconds, 2 minutes, and advanced. Cover ‘Who caused God?’, infinite regress, composition fallacy, God of the gaps, scientism, complex designer, one god further, evil, and hiddenness. Model a calm, charitable tone and end with a premise-locating checklist."
  }
